# CORE #11/#12 REGRESSION — `env[k] = v` and `env[k] OP= v` on a
# `Dict[K, V] &` (unique-borrow) param write THROUGH the caller's
# collection — every scalar-in-struct AND pointer-in-struct field
# stays coherent, including `count` (visible as `.len()`).
#
# AXIS PIN: Dict[String, int] (kind OrderedMap) · `&`-param root ·
# subscript-assign and compound `+=` · `.len()` observation of the
# scalar `count` field · read-back of values from the caller.
#
# RED-VERIFIED PRE-FIX (Round XXX Track G scout 2026-08-04, base
# `d00e71a0`): printed `0` for both `env.len()` and `d.len()` (should
# be `3`), even though `d["a"]`, `d["b"]`, `d["c"]` all read back the
# right values (1, 2, 3). Root: `lower_index_assign` and its compound
# sibling fell to `lower_expr(Identifier("env"))`, which auto-derefs
# a `&`-param MutPtr into a fresh stack local of the Dict struct;
# `emit_borrow_mut` then borrowed the STACK COPY and every setter's
# scalar-in-struct write (`count`, `entries_len`, `tombstones`) was
# silently DISCARDED when the copy died. Pointer fields (`keys` /
# `values` / `indices`) shared the caller's buffers, so writes THERE
# persisted — masking the scalar-loss on the legacy sparse table
# (correctness there depended only on the shared pointer arrays) and
# turning FATAL under D39 dense mode (`entries_len` is the probe-loop
# bound + the value written into `indices[insert_slot]`).
#
# Fix: `unique_borrow_param_ptr_operand` in `stmts/assigns.rs`
# returns the RAW MutPtr local for a `&`/`!`-param identifier
# object, so both the plain and compound index-assign arms pass the
# caller's pointer straight to the setter (mirrors the field-assign
# class — Core #4, one helper).

void mutate(Dict[String, int] &env):
    # env enters with "seed" already inserted by main (1 entry) —
    # `.len()` here observes the CALLER's scalar `count` too.
    env["a"] = 1
    env["b"] = 2
    env["c"] = 3
    env["a"] += 10        # compound (uses the sibling compound arm)
    print(env.len())      # 4  (seed + a + b + c) — pre-fix: 1
    print(env["a"])       # 11
    print(env["b"])       # 2
    print(env["c"])       # 3

void main():
    Dict[String, int] d = Dict[String, int]()
    d["seed"] = 99
    mutate(&d)
    # After returning, every scalar + pointer field must reflect
    # what mutate() did — the caller sees the same state.
    print(d.len())        # 4 — pre-fix: 1 (scalar `count` discarded)
    print(d["seed"])      # 99
    print(d["a"])         # 11
    print(d["b"])         # 2
    print(d["c"])         # 3
